"Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments (https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.5240)\nciteturn2academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.1 years ago; Title: Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments ... Berman, Ian N. Wakeling ... We present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments.\nTitle: Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\nAuthors: David R. Berman, Ian N. Wakeling\nDate: Sat Oct 19 15:48:04 2013\n\nWe present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. We show that CMDRR(n,k) exist for all allowed values of n and k apart from 4 exceptions and 31 possible exceptions. We show that a fully resolvable CMDRR(2n,0) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and a fully resolvable CMDRR(3n,n) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and n odd. We prove a product theorem for constructing CMDRR(n,k).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComplete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/1310.5240v1)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; ## Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments ... Berman, Ian N. Wakeling ... We present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments.\n# arXiv++ Combinatorics\n\nBrowse math.CO papers from arXiv\n\n## Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\n\nDavid R. Berman, Ian N. Wakeling\n\nPublished: 2013-10-19\n\nComments: 22 pages; Accepted October 2013 for publication in J. Combin. Math. Combin. Comput\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. We show that CMDRR(n,k) exist for all allowed values of n and k apart from 4 exceptions and 31 possible exceptions. We show that a fully resolvable CMDRR(2n,0) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and a fully resolvable CMDRR(3n,n) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and n odd. We prove a product theorem for constructing CMDRR(n,k).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDavid R. Berman - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/author/david-r-berman)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Berman ... 2024-06-30 v2 ... Wakeling ... We present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments.\n\n## Papers by David R. Berman\n\n2 paper(s) by this author · All BibTeX\n\n2024-06-30 v2\n\nDavid R. Berman, Lee O. Leonard\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nA 2-Person Game Decomposing 2-Manifolds\n\nTwo players play a game by alternately splitting a surface of a compact $2$-manifold along a simple closed curve that is not null-homotopic and attaching disks to the resulting boundary; the last player who can move wins. Starting from an orientable surface, the $G$-series is $01\\dot{2}\\dot{0}$ according to increasing genus. Starting from a nonorientable surface, the $G$-series is $012\\dot{4}60\\dot{3}$ according to increasing genus. Nim addition determines the $G$-values of the remaining compact $2$-manifolds.\n\n2013-10-19\n\nDavid R. Berman, Ian N. Wakeling\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nComplete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\n\nWe present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. We show that CMDRR(n,k) exist for all allowed values of n and k apart from 4 exceptions and 31 possible exceptions. We show that a fully resolvable CMDRR(2n,0) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and a fully resolvable CMDRR(3n,n) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and n odd. We prove a product theorem for constructing CMDRR(n,k).\n\ntournament--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComplete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments (https://combinatorialpress.com/jcmcc-articles/volume-095/complete-mixed-doubles-round-robin-tournaments/)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments ... We present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, \\( \\text{CMDRR}(n, k) \\), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments.\n\n## Journal of Combinatorial Mathematics and Combinatorial Computing[Input]\n\n  * Volume 095\n  * Pages: 65-86\n\n  * Research article\n\n# Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\n\n^{^{1}}^{}^{}, ^{^{2}}^{}\n\n^{1}Department of Computer Science University of North Carolina Wilmington Wilmington, NC 28403\n\n^{2}Qi Statistics Ltd. Penhales House, Ruscombe Berkshire RG10 9JN, UK ianQqistatistics.co.uk\n\n  * Published: 30/11/2015\n\n  * Download PDF\n  * Cite\n\n  * Copyright Link\n  * License\n\n#### Abstract\n\nWe present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, \\( \\text{CMDRR}(n, k) \\), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. We show that \\( \\text{CMDRR}(n, k) \\) exist for all allowed values of \\( n \\) and \\( k \\) apart from 4 exceptions and 31 possible exceptions. We show that a fully resolvable \\( \\text{CMDRR}(2n, 0) \\) exists for all \\( n \\geq 5 \\) and a fully resolvable \\( \\text{CMDRR}(3n, n) \\) exists for all \\( n \\geq 5 \\) and \\( n \\) odd. We prove a product theorem for constructing \\( \\text{CMDRR}(n, k) \\).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComplete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/1310.5240)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; Wakeling ... We present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. ... @misc{berman2013completemixeddoublesround, title={Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments}, author={David R.\nSubmitted 19 Oct 2013\n\n# Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\n\nUniversity of North Carolina Wilmington Qi Statistics Ltd.\n\nDR\n\nDavid R. Berman\n\nIN\n\nIan N. Wakeling\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. We show that CMDRR(n,k) exist for all allowed values of n and k apart from 4 exceptions and 31 possible exceptions. We show that a fully resolvable CMDRR(2n,0) exists for all n $\\ge$≥ 5 and a fully resolvable CMDRR(3n,n) exists for all n $\\ge$≥ 5 and n odd. We prove a product theorem for constructing CMDRR(n,k).\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{berman2013completemixeddoublesround, title={Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments}, author={David R. Berman and Ian N. Wakeling}, year={2013}, eprint={1310.5240}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.5240}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComplete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/258082408_Complete_Mixed_Doubles_Round_Robin_Tournaments)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Berman ... Wakeling ... We present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. ... As a consequence, we then establish that for h  2 (mod 4) and h ⩾ 10 a HSOLSSOM(hn) exists for all n ⩾ 5 except possibly for nϵE.\n\n# Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\n\n  * October 2013\n\n  * Source\n  * arXiv\n\nAuthors:\n\nDavid R. Berman\n\nDavid R. Berman\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nIan N. Wakeling\n\nIan N. Wakeling\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. We show that CMDRR(n,k) exist for all allowed values of n and k apart from 4 exceptions and 31 possible exceptions. We show that a fully resolvable CMDRR(2n,0) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and a fully resolvable CMDRR(3n,n) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and n odd. We prove a product theorem for constructing CMDRR(n,k).\n\n## Citations (0)\n\n## References (15)\n\nResearchGate has not been able to resolve any citations for this publication.\nK. Brayton】\n  * Don Coppersmith\n  * A.J. Hoffman\n\nView\n\nA few more incomplete self-orthogonal Latin squares and related designs\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 2000\n\n  * R. Julian R. Abel\n  * Frank E. Bennett\n  * H. Zhang\n  * Ledoux Zhu\n\n\nExistence of self-orthogonal Latin squares ISOLS(6m+2,2m)\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 1995\n\n  * Lie Zhu\n\nView\n\nSelf-orthogonal latin squares of all orders 𝑛≠2,3,6\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 1974\n  * B AM MATH SOC\n\n  * R. K. Brayton\n  * Donald Coppersmith\n\nHoley Steiner pentagon systems\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 1999\n  * J COMB DES\n\n  * R. Julian R. Abel\n  * Frank E. Bennett\n  * H. Zhang\n\n\nConstructing cyclic PBIBD(2)s through an optimization approach: Thirty‐two new cyclic designs\n\nArticle\n\n  * Dec 2004\n  * J COMB DES\n\n  * Luis B. Morales\n\nWe formulate the construction of cyclic partially balanced incomplete block designs with two associate classes (PBIBD(2)s) as a combinatorial optimization problem. We propose an algorithm based on tabu search to tackle the problem. Our algorithm constructed 32 new cyclic PBIBD(2)s. © 2004 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.\n\n\nThe spectrum of HSOLSSOM(hn) where h is even\n\nArticle\n\n  * Oct 1996\n  * DISCRETE MATH\n\n  * Frank E. Bennett\n  * Lie Zhu\n\nIn this paper, we describe a generalized product construction and a construction using Steiner pentagon systems to obtain holey self-orthogonal Latin squares with symmetric orthogonal mates (HSOLSSOM). We investigate the existence of HSOLSSOM(hn) for even h. We first improve the known result for h = 2 and show that a HSOLSSOM(2n) exists for all n ⩾ 5 except possibly for n ∈ E = {8, 10, 12, 14, 15, 16, 18, 20, 22, 24, 28, 32}. As a consequence, we then establish that for h  2 (mod 4) and h ⩾ 10 a HSOLSSOM(hn) exists for all n ⩾ 5 except possibly for nϵE. More conclusively, we show that for h  0 (mod 4), a HSOLSSOM(hn) exists if and only if n ⩾ 5. We are also able to apply our results to construct a unipotent SOLSSOM(62), the existence of which was previously unknown.\n\nExistence of frame SOLS of type\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 2002\n\n  * Yunqin