that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments.\nThe program fixes the partnerships and then exchanges them between games in a tabu search algorithm that seeks to optimize the opposition pairs incidence matrix (see [14]). The examples will be used in the next section as the basis of our recursive construction. ...\n\nComplete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\n\nArticle\n\n  * Oct 2013\n\n  * David R. Berman\n  * Ian N. Wakeling\n\nWe present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. We show that CMDRR(n,k) exist for all allowed values of n and k apart from 4 exceptions and 31 possible exceptions. We show that a fully resolvable CMDRR(2n,0) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and a fully resolvable CMDRR(3n,n) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and n odd. We prove a product theorem for constructing CMDRR(n,k).\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nConstruction of Super-Saturated Designs\n\nArticle\n\nConstructing some PBIBD(2)s by Tabu Search Algorithm\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Nov 2002\n\n  * Luis B. Morales\n\nSome papers have dealt with the construction of 2-concurrence designs as an optimization problem. In this paper we generalize these results to formulate also the construction of partially balanced incomplete block designs with 2 associate classes as a combinatorial optimization problem. We will propose an algorithm based on tabu search to construct many of these designs. Our algorithm was able to construct 51 resolvable and 71 non-resolvable PBIBD(2)s.\n\nView\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSelf-orthogonal latin squares (SOLS) (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/329786575_Self-orthogonal_latin_squares_SOLS)\nciteturn2search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.9 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Berman ... Wakeling\n\nWe present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. ... We show that there is a BARRDT(n) that is not a spouse avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments SAMDRR(n) for all n>4.\n## Citations (6)\n\n## References (0)\n\n... The total number of games is n(n − 1)/2. The existence of a resolvable SAMDRR(n) is equivalent to the existence of a self-orthogonal latin square of order n with a symmetric orthogonal mate (SOLSSOM) (see [3,10]). ...\n\n... In this section we give examples of CMDRR(n, k) for n ≤ 8, and also of a CMDRR(9, 3) and a CMDRR (10,2). Examples of SAMDRR(n) can be found in [3]. ...\n\n... For completeness we include the definition of a holey SOLS (see [10]). Suppose an HSOLS exists and CMDRR exist for each hole size. ...\n\nComplete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\n\nArticle\n\n  * Oct 2013\n\n  * David R. Berman\n  * Ian N. Wakeling\n\nWe present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. We show that CMDRR(n,k) exist for all allowed values of n and k apart from 4 exceptions and 31 possible exceptions. \nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nThe existence of 3 pairwise additive B(ν, 2,1) for any ν ≥ 6\n\nArticle\n\n  * Nov 2015\n\n  * K. Matsubara\n  * S. Kageyama\n\nThe existence of additive BIB designs and 2 pairwise additive BIB designs has been discussed with direct and recursive constructions in [6, 9]. In this paper, by reorganizing some methods of constructing pairwise additive BIB designs from other combinatorial structures, it is shown that 3 pairwise additive B(ν,2,l) can be constructed for any integer ν ≥ 6.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nBrother Avoiding Round Robin Doubles Tournaments II\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jul 2009\n\n  * David R. Berman\n  * Malcolm Greig\n  * Douglas D. Smith\n\nWe investigate brother avoiding round robin doubles tournaments (BARRDTs) and construct several infinite families. We show that there is a BARRDT(n) that is not a spouse avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments SAMDRR(n) for all n>4. For part I, see [D. R. Berman and D. D. Smith, Congr. Numerantium 179, 121–127 (2006; Zbl 1233.05110)].\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nMixed group divisible designs with three groups and block size 4\n\nArticle\n\n  * Sep 2010\n  * DISCRETE MATH\n\n  * Mingzhi Zhu\n  * Gennian Ge\n\nA group divisible design (GDD) with three groups and block size 4 is called even, odd, or mixed if the sizes of the non-empty intersections of any of its blocks with any of the three groups are always even, always odd, or always mixed. It has been shown that the necessary conditions for the existence of GDDs of these three types are also sufficient except possibly for the minimal case of mixed designs for group size 5t (t>1). In this paper, we complete the undetermined families of mixed GDDs using two constructions based on idempotent self-orthogonal Latin squares and skew Room squares.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDavid R. Berman's research works | University of North Carolina Wilmington, Wilmington (UNCW) and other places (https://www.researchgate.net/scientific-contributions/David-R-Berman-70980482)\nciteturn2search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; We present a new type of tournament design that we call a Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournament, give several examples, and prove a product theorem for constructing these designs. ... We show that there is a BARRDT(n) that is not a spouse avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments SAMDRR(n) for all n>4. ... This concept extends the multi-team generalized whist tournament designs introduced by Abel, Finizio, Greig, and Lewis and extends the idea of team tournaments introduced by Berman, McLaurin, and Smith by allowing more than two competing teams per game and relaxing the condition on the number of players sitting out each round.\n\n# David R. Berman’s research while affiliated with University of North Carolina Wilmington and other places\n\n## What is this page?\n\n* * *\n\nThis page lists works of an author who doesn't have a ResearchGate profile or hasn't added the works to their profile yet. It is automatically generated from public (personal) data to further our legitimate goal of comprehensive and accurate scientific recordkeeping. If you are this author and want this page removed, please let us know.\n\n## Publications (12)\n\n* * *\n\nBalanced equitable mixed doubles round-robin tournaments\n\n  * Article\n\nJanuary 2013\n\n·\n\n49 Reads\n\n·\n\n1 Citation\n\nDavid R. Berman\n\n·\n\nDouglas D. Smith\n\n* * *\n\nMitchell tournaments\n\n  * Article\n\nJanuary 2012\n\n·\n\n13 Reads\n\nDavid R. Berman\n\n·\n\nDouglas D. Smith\n\nWe present a new type of tournament design that we call a Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournament, give several examples, and prove a product theorem for constructing these designs. The existence of these tournaments provides answers to questions recently asked by Ian Anderson in reference to earlier work by J. T. Mitchell.\n\n[Button: Read more]\n\n* * *\n\nBrother Avoiding Round Robin Doubles Tournaments II\n\n  * Article\n\nJuly 2009\n\n·\n\n97 Reads\n\n·\n\n1 Citation\n\nArs Combinatoria\n\nDavid R. Berman\n\n·\n\nMalcolm Greig\n\n·\n\nDouglas D. Smith\n\nWe investigate brother avoiding round robin doubles tournaments (BARRDTs) and construct several infinite families. We show that there is a BARRDT(n) that is not a spouse avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments SAMDRR(n) for all n>4. For part I, see [D. R. Berman and D. D. Smith, Congr. Numerantium 179, 121–127 (2006; Zbl 1233.05110)].\n\n[Button: Read more]\n\n* * *\n\nSplittable whist tournament designs\n\n  * Article\n\nJanuary 2008\n\n·\n\n4 Reads\n\nCongressus Numerantium\n\nDavid R. Berman\n\n·\n\nNorman J. Finizo\n\n·\n\nDouglas D. Smith\n\n* * *\n\nNested BIBDs from affine planes\n\n  * Article\n\nJuly 2004\n\n·\n\n25 Reads\n\nArs Combinatoria\n\nDavid R. Berman\n\nFor any prime power q, there exists an affine plane of order q. The complement of an affine plane is a balanced incomplete block design (BIBD) with block size q 2 - q. In this note, a proof is given that the blocks can be split into sub-blocks to form a nested BIBD with parameters (q 2, q 2 +q, q 3+q 2, q 2 - 1, q 2 - q, q-1). Alternatively, this is a generalized tournament design with one game each round, involving q teams, each team with q - 1 players.\n\n[Button: Read more]\n\n* * *\n\nRanking whist players\n\n  * Article\n\nJune 2004\n\n·\n\n34 Reads\n\n·\n\n2 Citations\n\nDiscrete Mathematics\n\nDavid R. Berman\n\n·\n\nSandra C. McLaurin\n\n·\n\nDouglas D. Smith\n\nThe study of ranking for player in round-robin tournaments is discussed. For dealing with the ranking problem for round-robin tournaments, likelihood principle is developed. The difficulties arise in the whist tournament situation points out that the strongest and the weakest player must be partners. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLatin Squares and Their Applications (https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/77164/1/2.pdf)\nciteturn2search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.7 years ago; The third paper, Berman and Smith(2012), discusses what the authors call a Mitchell tournament which is a variant of the famous type of tournament known as a spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tennis tournament. ... Finally, it is worth mentioning that scheduling a Round robin tournament for 2m teams or players is equivalent to finding a 1-factorization(see Section 8.3 for the definition) of the complete graph K2m as has been pointed out in Gelling and Odeh(1974).\n(See Section 2.6 for the definition of the latter.)\n\nThe second, due to Preece and Phillips(2002), concerns the problem of scheduling bowls tournaments. A bowls team consists of 4n players, n ≥ 4, n playing lead position, n playing second position, n playing third position and n playing skip position. The problem is to assign the players to n rinks in n successive games in such a way that each rink contains one player for each position and no two players appear together more than once in any given rink. Solving this problem is equivalent to finding three mutually orthogonal Latin squares of order n. Adding the restriction that each player appears exactly once in each rink yields an enhanced problem that is equivalent to finding four mutually orthogonal Latin squares of order n.\n\nThe third paper, Berman and Smith(2012), discusses what the authors call a Mitchell tournament which is a variant of the famous type of tournament known as a spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tennis tournament. The conditions for a Mitchell tournament differ from those for the type just mentioned only in the fact that a tournament of the latter type is not spouse-avoiding. For the construction, the authors use one square A which is column-latin and a second square B which is row-latin and such that A and B are orthogonal.\n\nFinally, it is worth mentioning that scheduling a Round robin tournament for 2m teams or players is equivalent to finding a 1-factorization(see Section 8.3 for the definition) of the complete graph K2m as has been pointed out in Gelling and Odeh(1974). The teams are the vertices of K2m, the games are the edges, the rounds are the 1-factors.\n\nThe topic of devising games tournaments is still an active one so the above situations are not the only ones. In particular, I.Anderson(1997) has written a book on the subject and Berman has written several relevant papers on tournaments in addition to that mentioned above. [See, for example, Berman and Smith(2013) and D.R.Berman’s computer home page.]--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRonald Vetter, Ph. D. (https://people.uncw.edu/vetterr/grid/proposal/UNCW_All_Bio_Sketches.pdf)\nciteturn2search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Berman, D.R., McLaurin, S.C., and Smith, D.D, (2004) Ranking whist players, Discrete ... Berman, D.R., McLaurin, S.C., and Smith, D.D, (2000) Generalized tournament designs, ... spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round-robin tournaments, Congressus Numerantium, Vol. ... complete Hilbert functions, Vol.\ntournaments, Congressus Numerantium, to appear. \n3. Berman, D.R., (2004) Nested BIBDs from affine planes, Ars Combinatoria, to appear.  \n4. Berman, D.R., McLaurin, S.C., and Smith, D.D, (2000) Generalized tournament designs, \nCongressus Numerantium, Vol. 142, pp. 33-40.  \n5. Berman, D.R., McLaurin, S.C., and Smith, D.D, (1999) Team tournaments, Congressus \nNumerantium, Vol. 137, pp. 33-46.  \n6. McLaurin, S.C., Berman, D.R., and Smith, D.D, (1998) Fairness in whist tournaments, \nCongressus Numerantium, Vol. 130, pp. 189-198.  \n7. Berman, D.R., McLaurin, S.C., and Smith, D.D, (1997) Additional results on fairness in \nspouse-avoiding mixed doubles round-robin tournaments, Congressus Numerantium, Vol. \n123, pp. 181-191. \n8. Berman, D.R., McLaurin, S.C., and Smith, D.D, (1996) Fairness in Spouse-Avoiding Mixed \nDoubles Round-Robin Tournaments, Vol. 119, pp. 65-72. \n9. Berman, D.R., (1981) The number of generators of a colength N ideal in a power series ring, \nJournal of Algebra, Vol. 73, No. 1, pp. 156-166. \n10. Berman, D.R., (1977) Simplicity of a vector space of forms; finiteness of the number of \ncomplete Hilbert functions, Vol. 45, No. 1, pp. 88-93. \n \nd. Synergistic Activities \nDevelopment and maintenance of softwa